      "title": "Atlas framework introduces trajectory sensemaking for long-horizon autonomous AI agents",
      "summary": "Researchers introduced Atlas, an interface framework designed to help humans evaluate complex agent runs conducted without real-time oversight. As autonomous agents execute extensive multi-step workflows, Atlas organizes lengthy trajectory histories into navigable structures for post-hoc sensemaking.",
      "key_facts": [
        "Addresses asynchronous sensemaking for unsupervised, long-horizon agent runs.",
        "Structures multi-step trajectories so users can audit unmonitored execution."
      ],
      "design_implication": "Returning to an agent workspace after several hours, a user faces an overwhelming backlog of actions and decisions. Linear chat transcripts fail when reviewing long-running autonomous tasks. Interfaces must shift toward hierarchical trajectory maps that summarize progress while surfacing branch points for validation. Try structuring background agent activity logs into inspectable checkpoint cards rather than continuous text feeds.",
      "implication_lens": "pattern",
      "visual_need": "illustrate",
      "source_name": "arXiv cs.HC",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30548",
      "source_date": "2026-10-23",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Atlas helps humans evaluate long-running agent workflows completed without real-time monitoring.",
          "quote": "When researchers return to a long-horizon run, they must evaluate work they did not monitor in real time."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Autonomous agent execution requires new interfaces for human sensemaking.",
          "quote": "Yet scaling scientific execution does not automatically scale researchers' ability to make sense of what agents produce."
        }
      ],
